AtonixCorp Cloud Platform Implementation Specification
This specification converts the approved AtonixCorp Cloud wireframes into an implementation-ready product, frontend, backend, platform, security, quality-assurance, and operations reference. It covers the complete persistent sidebar: Dashboard, Compute, Containers, Container Groups, Networking, Load Balancers, Storage, Databases, AI/ML, Identity & Access, Monitoring, Marketplace, Cost & Billing, Support, and Settings. Every module must preserve organization, region, environment, actor, authorization, and correlation context as users navigate across the console.
12. Global Cloud Shell, Navigation, and Shared Experience
Navigation order. Dashboard → Compute → Containers → Container Groups → Networking → Load Balancers → Storage → Databases → AI/ML → Identity & Access → Monitoring → Marketplace → Cost & Billing → Support → Settings.
Global shell. Provide the AtonixCorp Cloud wordmark, organization selector, region selector, environment selector, universal search, notification center, help, user menu, breadcrumb, page title, contextual actions, persistent left navigation, and a responsive content canvas.
Context contract. Every API request, event, audit record, stream, and background operation must carry tenantId, organizationId, region, environment, actorId, authorization context, correlationId, requestId, and resource scope.
Shared page states. Implement loading skeletons, empty state, provisioning, syncing, healthy, scaling, degraded, partial outage, maintenance, failed, offline with cached data, permission denied with deny reason, and destructive-action confirmation.
Shared interaction standards. Use cursor pagination, stable sorting, filter chips, saved views, bulk actions, resumable operations, optimistic feedback only when safe, cancelable long-running queries, localized timestamps and currency, keyboard navigation, visible focus, semantic labels, no color-only status, and WCAG-aligned contrast.
Frontend architecture. Use typed routes, a shared design system, generated API client, query cache, normalized errors, feature flags, localization, virtualized large lists, progressive charts, and a stream client for server-sent events or WebSocket updates.
Canonical resource envelope. id, tenantId, organizationId, region, environment, name, type, status, labels, tags, createdAt, updatedAt, createdBy, version, links, permissions, and freshness.
Operation envelope. operationId, resourceId, state, percent, currentStep, startedAt, updatedAt, completedAt, warnings, error, retryable, correlationId, and rollbackAvailable.
13. Dashboard and Cloud Command Center
Purpose. The Dashboard must answer: “What is happening across my entire cloud platform right now?” It aggregates operational, security, cost, reliability, support, and AI signals without replacing module-specific detail pages.
Summary cards. Implement cards for Compute, Containers, Networking, Storage, Databases, Load Balancers, IAM, Monitoring, AI/ML, Marketplace, Cost & Billing, and Support. Each card contains status, primary metric, short trend, freshness, quick action, and permission-aware drill-through.
Insight panels. Provide Resource & Capacity, Network & Traffic, Service Health, Security & IAM Posture, Monitoring & Reliability, and Cost & Optimization panels. Panels must support region/environment filtering and preserve the selected time range.
AI Cloud Intelligence. Correlate Compute, Containers, Networking, Load Balancers, Databases, Storage, IAM, Monitoring, Cost, and Support. Show confidence, evidence, affected resources, recommended action, approval requirement, and audit link. AI is advisory and cannot bypass RBAC, policy, budget controls, or human approval.
Operations rail. Include quick actions, critical/warning/approval/system notifications, region and environment selectors, recent activity, and operation progress. Sensitive actions require step-up authentication.
Core APIs. GET /dashboard/summary; GET /dashboard/health; GET /dashboard/activity; GET /dashboard/recommendations; GET /operations; GET /notifications; POST /dashboard/layouts.
Acceptance. Cards and panels load independently; partial failures do not blank the page; data freshness is explicit; cached mode is labeled; permission-denied modules show a safe explanation; dashboard layout preferences persist per user and workspace.
14. Compute, Containers, and Container Groups
Compute. Implement virtual-machine inventory, launch wizard, instance detail, images, instance families, disks, networking, firewall/security groups, SSH/access controls, start/stop/reboot/resize, snapshots, monitoring, cost, and scheduled operations. Deploy immutable image versions and require confirmation for termination and destructive disk actions.
Containers overview. Provide serverless container hosting with families General, CPU-optimized, Memory-optimized, GPU, and Spot. The inventory shows image digest, status, CPU/memory, restarts, zone, environment, owner, cost, and tags. Actions include restart, scale, logs, metrics, manifest, and delete.
Deploy Container wizard. Image selection → runtime configuration → networking → scaling → deployment strategy → review and deploy. Validate signatures, vulnerabilities, SBOM, Vault references, ports, health probes, VPC/subnet/security-group access, replica limits, autoscaling thresholds, routing strategy, and cost before creation.
Container node. Tabs: Overview, Metrics, Logs, Events, Networking, Storage, Security, Cost, YAML/Manifest. Stream CPU, memory, network, disk, request, latency, logs, events, traffic weight, runtime threats, and monthly forecast. Manifest editing requires scoped RBAC and versioned diff.
Container groups. Support replicated multi-zone workloads, desired/available replicas, topology, instances, traffic, deployments, security, cost, and rollbacks. Actions include scale, update image, rollout, rollback, pause, resume, and delete.
Autoscaling. Support metric, schedule, and predictive modes; min/max replicas; cooldowns; scale-up/down stabilization; CPU, memory, request, queue, and custom metrics; per-zone limits; budget cap; deployment protection; and approval above configured thresholds. Include live activity, event evidence, correlation IDs, simulation, projected SLO, cost, thrashing risk, and audit history.
Core APIs. GET/POST /compute/instances; POST /compute/instances/{id}/actions; GET /containers; POST /containers; GET /container-groups; POST /container-groups; PUT /autoscaling/policies/{id}; POST /autoscaling/policies/{id}/simulate; GET /operations/{id}/stream.
Events. instance.provisioning|running|stopped|failed; container.created|healthy|degraded|restarted; group.scaled|rollout.started|rolled_back; autoscaling.triggered|blocked|completed.
15. Networking and Load Balancers
Networking overview. Show VPCs, subnets, security groups, load balancers, firewalls, latency, traffic, routing mode, connected services, cost, and AI recommendations.
Create VPC wizard. Basic settings → subnet planning → routing → security → review. Support automatic or manual CIDR planning, public/private subnets, multi-zone distribution, static/dynamic routing, internet/NAT gateways, route propagation, default security groups, firewall rules, flow logs, overlap validation, and cost estimate.
VPC detail. Tabs: Overview, Subnets, Routing Table, Security Groups, Firewall, Flow Logs, Cost. Show attached resources, route status, SG risk, firewall events, accepted/rejected traffic, top talkers, suspicious patterns, and forecast.
Load balancers. Support HTTP/HTTPS, TCP, UDP, internal, and external schemes. Creation flow: basic settings → networking → listeners → target groups → routing rules → review. Listener configuration must support TLS certificates, redirect, path/host routing, session persistence, and health-check ports.
Target groups and traffic. Register instance, container, or IP targets; configure HTTP/TCP/command health checks, thresholds, deregistration delay, round-robin, least-connections, weighted, sticky, and canary routing. Display target health, latency, traffic, and zone.
Certificates and protection. Support managed, ACME, and uploaded certificates; expiry monitoring; allowed TLS versions and cipher suites; DDoS protection; security-group and firewall linkage.
AI networking. Detect risky routes, overlapping CIDRs, unused subnets, exposed ports, unhealthy targets, latency anomalies, certificate expiry, DDoS patterns, and cost waste. Every recommendation shows evidence and remains approval-controlled.
Core APIs/events. POST /vpcs; GET /vpcs/{id}; POST /subnets; POST /routes; POST /security-groups; POST /load-balancers; POST /target-groups; POST /certificates; network.route.changed; firewall.blocked; loadbalancer.target.unhealthy; certificate.expiring.
16. Storage and Databases
Storage. Implement object, block, and file storage inventories; bucket/volume/share creation; capacity, IOPS, throughput, latency, lifecycle, versioning, replication, encryption, access policies, snapshots, restore, retention, immutability, cost, and monitoring. Destructive deletion requires dependency checks, typed confirmation, and retention-policy evaluation.
Database overview. Unify Managed SQL, Managed NoSQL, and Managed Cache in one inventory with engine, version, region, status, storage, connections, environment, cost, tags, and actions.
Managed SQL creation. Engine/version → basic settings → compute/storage → high availability → networking/security → review. Support PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, and SQL Server; instance classes; SSD/HDD; optional IOPS; single/multi-zone; failover; retention; private endpoints; IAM/DB users; allowed CIDRs; and KMS encryption.
SQL operations. Tabs: Overview, Metrics, Connections, Backups, Query Console, Security, Networking, Cost. Show CPU, memory, disk I/O, query latency, QPS, errors, active sessions, slow/blocking queries, backups, snapshots, restore, roles, audit logs, endpoint, SGs, and forecast. Query console must protect production with permissions, timeout, result limits, and audit logging.
NoSQL. Support document and key-value engines, node class/count, storage per node, sharding, replication, backup, document query console, collections/tables, hot-partition metrics, access control, and cost.
Cache. Support Redis and Memcached, node size/count, primary/replica, VPC, SGs, encryption, hit rate, latency, memory, operations, failover, and safe key summaries without unrestricted production browsing.
AI data insights. Identify over/under-provisioning, slow queries, connection spikes, hot partitions, cache inefficiency, backup gaps, storage waste, and cost opportunities. Recommendations must include confidence, performance impact, and rollback plan.
Core APIs/events. POST /storage/buckets|volumes|shares; POST /databases/sql|nosql|cache; POST /databases/{id}/scale; POST /backups; POST /restores; database.failover.started|completed; backup.completed|failed; storage.capacity.threshold.
17. AI/ML, Identity & Access, and Security
AI/ML. Implement models, datasets, training jobs, inference endpoints, artifacts, versions, GPU capacity, experiments, deployment, monitoring, security, and cost. Model deployment must validate compatibility, artifact signature, resource capacity, endpoint security, autoscaling, expected latency, and estimated cost.
IAM overview. Provide Users, Groups, Roles (RBAC), Policies (ABAC), Service Accounts, API Keys, Sessions, Authentication, and Audit Logs. Deny by default and enforce authorization on the server for every request.
Users and groups. Support invite/create, password or SSO, MFA requirement, role/group/policy assignment, suspension, deactivation, MFA reset, last activity, and session review. Groups provide bulk role and policy assignment.
Roles and policies. Provide predefined and custom roles, effective-permission preview, deny reasons, policy validation, resource/action/condition statements, environment attributes, organization/region/resource scopes, versioning, and change approval.
Service accounts and API keys. Separate non-human identities from users. Display a key secret once; store only a secure hash; support scopes, expiry, allowed IPs, rotation, revocation, last use, owner, and anomaly detection.
Sessions and authentication. Support OIDC/SAML SSO, MFA, secure cookies, short-lived sessions, CSRF protection, device trust, IP controls, session termination, re-authentication, and step-up authentication for sensitive changes.
AI access analysis. Detect unused privileges, excessive access, suspicious key usage, impossible travel, privilege escalation, dormant roles, and policy misconfiguration. Findings must be explainable and reviewable.
Core APIs/events. POST /iam/users; POST /iam/groups; POST /iam/roles; POST /iam/policies; POST /iam/service-accounts; POST /iam/api-keys; POST /sessions/{id}/terminate; identity.created; role.assigned; policy.changed; key.rotated|revoked; authentication.failed.
18. Monitoring, Marketplace, Cost & Billing, and Support
Monitoring. Implement Metrics, Logs, Traces, Alerts, Dashboards, Service Map, SLOs, Error Budgets, Health, retention, ingestion health, and cost. Correlate tenant, resource, region, environment, trace ID, deployment ID, operation ID, and version. Long-running searches are asynchronous and cancelable.
Alerting. Support threshold, anomaly, absence-of-data, composite, latency, error-rate, and saturation rules; routing, maintenance windows, silences, deduplication, escalation, and optional policy-authorized remediation.
Marketplace. Catalog verified VM images, container images, AI models, datasets, blueprints, partner services, and tools. Include ratings, popularity, compatibility, versions, signatures, SBOM, vulnerability status, publisher, cost estimate, and deployment target. Publishing flow: type → metadata → upload → verification → publish.
Blueprint deployment. Validate multi-resource dependencies, IAM permission, region capacity, network plan, estimated cost, rollback feasibility, and signed template before orchestrating Compute, Containers, Networking, Storage, Databases, and Load Balancers.
Cost & Billing. Provide spend by service, account, owner, region, environment, tag, resource, and time; budgets, forecasts, anomalies, invoices, allocation, unit economics, and optimization. Recommendations must show expected savings, confidence, performance risk, approval path, and affected resources.
Support. Implement Tickets, Incidents, SLA, Status Pages, Escalations, Knowledge Base, attachments, communications, root-cause analysis, cost credits, and audit history. Ticket creation: category → details → evidence → review. Incidents support investigating, identified, mitigating, resolved, impact, linked tickets, customer and engineering communications, and post-incident analysis.
SLA and status. Track service uptime, latency/error targets, error budgets, violations, incident correlation, regional/service status, maintenance, public/internal messages, and credits where policy applies.
AI assistance. Monitoring, Marketplace, Cost, and Support AI must be advisory, evidence-based, permission-aware, and auditable. It may classify, correlate, forecast, identify duplicates, suggest root causes, and recommend actions but may not publish, charge, terminate, or change production without policy authorization.
Core APIs/events. GET /telemetry/metrics|logs|traces; POST /alerts/rules; GET /marketplace/items; POST /marketplace/publish; POST /blueprints/{id}/deploy; GET /billing/spend|forecast; POST /budgets; POST /support/tickets; POST /incidents; POST /status/updates; alert.fired|resolved; budget.threshold.exceeded; ticket.created|escalated; incident.updated|resolved.
19. Settings, Administration, and Platform Governance
Permanent settings. Settings are always available and never use onboarding or empty-state gating. Sections: General, Organization Identity, Regions and Environments, Compute Defaults, Container Defaults, Network Defaults, Storage Defaults, Database Defaults, AI/ML Defaults, IAM, Monitoring, Marketplace Publishing, Billing, Support, Integrations, Feature Flags, Data Residency, Retention, Audit Logs, and Danger Zone.
Administrative controls. Provide organization branding, regional enablement, quotas, default tags, encryption keys, agent channels, notification routes, maintenance windows, policy defaults, support plan, invoice profile, and service limits.
Integrations. Show health, scopes, owner, last sync, retry, rotate credential, disable, and disconnect. Credentials are stored in the secrets service; browser storage and analytics must never contain secret values.
Audit. Store immutable actor, action, resource, result, timestamp, tenant, region, source IP, user agent, correlation ID, before/after metadata, approval chain, and integrity proof. Support filters, export, retention, and legal hold.
Safeguards. Require step-up authentication for sensitive settings, dual approval for policy bypass, typed confirmation for destructive actions, dependency preview, delayed deletion where appropriate, recovery windows, and complete audit coverage.
20. Backend Architecture, Contracts, and Async Operations
Service architecture. Use an API gateway plus domain services for Identity, Compute, Containers, Networking, Load Balancing, Storage, Databases, AI/ML, Telemetry, Marketplace, Billing, Support, Notifications, Operations, and Audit.
Async platform. Use an event bus, workflow/job engine, scheduler, stream gateway, notification service, and dead-letter handling. Every mutating command returns a stable operationId and supports idempotency keys.
Error contract. code, message, userMessage, fieldErrors, correlationId, retryable, remediationUrl. Never render raw stack traces or provider secrets.
Pagination and queries. Use cursor pagination, stable sort keys, explicit filters, total estimate where available, request cancellation, query timeout, and export jobs for large result sets.
Events. Version events through a schema registry. Include eventId, eventType, occurredAt, producer, tenant, region, environment, resource, actor, correlationId, causationId, data, and schemaVersion.
Secrets and encryption. Persist secret references only; encrypt in transit and at rest; redact logs, traces, errors, exports, and analytics; rotate and expire credentials; separate tenant key material where required.
Compatibility. Prefer additive API evolution, explicit version headers, compatibility tests for agents/CLIs, migration rehearsal, and rollback-safe schema changes.
21. Security, Quality, Testing, and Release Gates
Security baseline. OIDC/SAML SSO, MFA, short sessions, secure cookies, CSRF protection, resource-scoped RBAC/ABAC, deny by default, encryption, signed builds, provenance, dependency pinning, SBOM, secret scanning, branch protection, artifact signatures, and release attestations.
Accessibility. Meet WCAG-aligned contrast and semantics; support keyboard operations, focus order, screen-reader announcements, reduced motion, scalable text, and non-color status cues.
Performance. Use paginated/virtualized data, progressive chart rendering, stream backpressure, cached metadata with freshness, lazy module loading, and explicit performance budgets for shell, lists, charts, and live consoles.
Testing pyramid. Unit tests for logic and components; contract tests for APIs/events; provider integration tests; end-to-end tests for each sidebar module; load tests for telemetry, queries, operations, and WebSocket/SSE streams; chaos tests for job retries, regional failure, failover, and delayed dependencies.
Required end-to-end journeys. Launch VM; deploy container; create and autoscale container group; create VPC/subnet/SG; create load balancer and canary target routing; create storage and restore snapshot; create SQL/NoSQL/cache; deploy model endpoint; invite user and rotate key; investigate alert; publish/deploy marketplace item; set budget; create/escalate support ticket; publish incident update; inspect audit entry.
Release gates. Tests pass; no unresolved critical vulnerability; migrations validated; rollback rehearsed; SLOs, dashboards, alerts, runbooks, support ownership, feature-flag plan, capacity plan, data-residency review, and disaster-recovery evidence approved.
22. Cloud Platform Definition of Done
•	Every sidebar module, list, detail view, wizard, empty state, populated state, live state, error state, permission state, offline state, and destructive confirmation is implemented.
•	All navigation preserves organization, region, environment, filters, authorization, and correlation context.
•	API schemas, event schemas, operations, authorization rules, audit coverage, telemetry, dashboards, alerts, and ownership are documented and contract-tested.
•	Provisioning, retries, cancellation, timeout, partial failure, stale data, reconnect, rollback, and recovery behavior are verified.
•	No secret value appears in UI logs, telemetry, exports, browser storage, or analytics; sensitive actions require approval and step-up authentication as defined.
•	Accessibility, responsive behavior, localization, performance budgets, supported browsers, and required end-to-end journeys pass.
•	AI recommendations are explainable, confidence-scored, evidence-linked, advisory, permission-aware, and fully auditable.
•	Operational runbooks, escalation paths, support coverage, migration plans, disaster recovery, rollback procedures, regional capacity, and production ownership are ready.
•	Final navigation acceptance: a user can move coherently from Dashboard through every sidebar module and back without losing operational context, while every action remains secure, auditable, recoverable, and consistent with the approved wireframes.
